The jobs report · September 2026
Payrolls rose by an estimated 29,000 in September, a change too small for the survey to tell from none
Economists surveyed by Reuters expected payrolls to rise by 90,000. The Bureau of Labor Statistics said payrolls and the 4.2 percent unemployment rate both changed little. July and August together are 60,000 lower than previously reported.
+29,000 payrolls in September
4.2 % unemployment rate
60,000 lower July and August, revised
What happened
The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little.
Private payrolls rose by 46,000 and government payrolls fell by 17,000, with 10,600 of that in local government outside education.
Kevin Hassett is the director of the National Economic Council, the title Yahoo Finance gave him in a May 11, 2026 article. He told reporters outside the White House on October 2 that “private sector employment is still booming.” His account of the jobs numbers was “What's happened is because we've reduced federal employment by 300,000 workers, then the numbers, uh, are a little bit lower if you don't account for that.” He also said that “50% of the job creation was hiring federal government workers” over “the last two years,” and he did not name the months.
Federal payrolls were 3,009,000 in December 2024 and 2,682,000 in September 2026, a decline of 327,000. September's own federal change was a drop of 1,000, from 2,683,000 in August. The 327,000 is the decline since December 2024. The 1,000 is September, so that longer decline does not account for this month's payroll change. From January 2023 to January 2025, a window he did not name, federal payrolls rose 130,000 while total payrolls rose 3,492,000, about 3.7 percent of the gain, not half. September's private payrolls rose by 46,000.
The bureau also revised the two prior months down by 60,000 together: August from a gain of 162,000 to 133,000, and July from a gain of 21,000 to a loss of 10,000.
The establishment survey counts jobs on employer payrolls, and the household survey counts people. The payroll figure comes from the first and the unemployment rate from the second, so the two can move differently in one month.
The Federal Reserve's two goals are maximum employment and price stability. On September 16 the Federal Open Market Committee raised the target range for the federal funds rate by 1/4 percentage point. The committee's goals statement says it “considers a wide range of indicators” in assessing maximum employment. This report's small payroll gain and little-changed unemployment are evidence the committee will weigh on October 27-28, and the October decision is not known. The next employment report, for October, is scheduled for November 6. On prices, the personal consumption expenditures price index for August, whose annual change is how the committee measures inflation, was up 3.4 percent from a year earlier. This report put average hourly earnings up 3.0 percent over the past 12 months.
Chris Phelan, chair of the President's Council of Economic Advisers, argued that the central bank's decision to raise rates last month was a mistake because inflation is already coming down. The Federal Open Market Committee's September 16 statement says “Inflation remains elevated.” Philip N. Jefferson, a vice chair of the Federal Reserve Board, said on October 1 that he supported that decision because he saw it as the appropriate policy to pursue the Fed's dual mandate. He also said he sees “economic activity and labor market conditions remaining broadly solid while inflation remains above our target.”
Reuters wrote that the slowdown was “almost taking another interest rate hike from the Federal Reserve this month off the table”. The same dispatch said financial markets initially lowered bets on a rate hike at the October 27-28 meeting to 13 percent before lifting them to about 23 percent, little changed from Thursday, CME Group's FedWatch tool showed.
The catch
USA Today headlined the release “September jobs report shows slowdown in hiring, unemployment ticks up”. The 29,000 is a net change in payroll jobs, not a count of hires, and it is under about 122,000, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant. Hires, “all additions to the payroll” during the month, are counted in a separate survey.
The rounded unemployment rate rose from 4.1 to 4.2. The bureau's summary says payrolls and that rate both “changed little”.
By the household survey's estimates, employment rose by 406,000, which is under the 650,000 that survey needs before a change is statistically significant. The labor force, the employed plus the unemployed, rose by 485,000. The unemployment rate is the number unemployed as a percent of the labor force. The labor force grew by more than employment did, so the count of unemployed rose by 78,000 and the rate went from 4.1 to 4.2 percent.
Most of that tenth is rounding. Before rounding, the rate “increased slightly from 4.141% to 4.175%”, the St. Louis Fed wrote on October 2, a rise of about 0.03 point. The rate has stayed between 4.1 percent and 4.3 percent since March.
The bureau named one group whose rate increased: Black workers, from 6.0 percent in August to 7.0 percent in September. A year earlier it was 7.6 percent.
Where this sits
Jobs gained and lost each month October 2025 to September 2026 · Seasonally adjusted
September: +29,000 jobs . Inspect the source data
Bureau of Labor Statistics payroll counts, from the St. Louis Fed's FRED database as published October 3, 2026; later releases may revise the counts.
Read the values and calculation
Each bar subtracts the previous month's total jobs from that month's total. The database reports totals in thousands.
