 succeeded in 0ms:
Held-list items are removed. Earlier-report labels follow each item.

**1. The seven questions**

1. What happened: **Answered**, no effort. "The Bureau of Labor Statistics reported on Friday, October 2, 2026, that payroll employment rose by 29,000 in September and the unemployment rate was 4.2 percent, and said both changed little."
2. Why it matters: **Partly.** The page never says what a jobs report is for. The closest is "This report's small payroll gain and little-changed unemployment are evidence the committee will weigh on October 27-28".
3. Wrong reading: **Answered**, some work. "it is under about 122,000, the monthly change the bureau says is statistically significant." "Most of that tenth is rounding."
4. Two surveys: **Answered.** "The payroll figure comes from the first and the unemployment rate from the second".
5. What changed: **Answered.** "It is below the bureau's average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months."
6. Unresolved: **Answered.** "The payroll figures for August and September are preliminary". "the October decision is not known."
7. Next questions: **Partly.** "Who the 4.2 percent left out" gets only "Black workers, from 6.0 percent in August to 7.0 percent in September."

**2. Stalls and misreads**

- (repeat, r2) The headline's "too small for the survey to tell from none" comes before any survey is named.
- (repeat, r2) "statistically significant" is never defined.
- (repeat, r4) "said both changed little" sits beside the 122,000 bar. I could not tell whether it is a verdict or just wording.
- (repeat, r1) "employment rose by 406,000, which is under the 650,000 that survey needs." It rose, yet it is under the bar.
- (repeat, r0) "Most of that tenth is rounding." "That tenth" has no antecedent.
- (repeat, r6) "so the count of unemployed rose by 78,000". The 78,000 has no earlier mention.
- (repeat, r0) The averages 45,000, 51,000, 68,000 and 9,700 come with nothing on how they relate.
- (new) The 68,000 and 9,700 are computed, then repeated as "The Associated Press reported an average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year, against 9,700".
- (repeat, r0) "federal funds rate" is unexplained.
- (repeat, r0) "Hires ... are counted in a separate survey." That survey is never named.
- (repeat, r3) "from the St. Louis Fed's FRED database as published October 3, 2026". FRED is unexplained, and the release was October 2.
- (repeat, r5) "Seasonally adjusted" is never explained.
- (repeat, r3) "incomplete returns" read as tax returns.
- (repeat, r10c) "then the numbers, uh, are a little bit lower". "The numbers" has no referent.
- (new) "From January 2023 to January 2025, a window he did not name". Why this window was chosen is not said.
- (repeat, r10c) "September's 29,000 is under the first two figures." I could not tell which figures.
- (new) "The same dispatch said financial markets initially lowered bets on a rate hike ... to 13 percent before lifting them to about 23 percent, little changed from Thursday". I could not tell which comparison "little changed" makes.
- (new) "September's hiring change is not known yet." It conflicts with the 29,000 payroll change just before it.
- (new) "The layoffs and discharges rate was 1.0 percent, 1.1 percent, and 1.0 percent in those months." Nothing says what the rate measures.
- (repeat, r7c) The October 2025 loss of 140,000, the chart's biggest drop, is never mentioned in the text.
- (repeat, r0) The Black workers rate is the only group named, with no reason given.
- (repeat, r0) The PCE and earnings sentences have no stated link to jobs.

**3. Written for the project, or by a machine**

- (repeat, r0) "The release states no cause for the 29,000 change." A document is the subject, and it answers a question I had not asked.
- (repeat, r10c) "the title Yahoo Finance gave him in a May 11, 2026 article". The outlet and its date stand where the fact could.
- (repeat, r1) "60,000 lower" appears in the dek, the panel and the body. "29,000" recurs many times before "The catch".
- (new) Line 58 repeats one comparison five times ("September's 29,000 is under roughly 50,000 ... The three-month average of 51,000 is above zero jobs a month"). It reads as generated.
- (new) "September's private payrolls rose by 46,000" repeats the "What happened" paragraph, inside the federal-employment paragraph.
- (repeat, r3) "Where this sits" reads as an interface label.
- (repeat, r7) The Source history lines are the project's bookkeeping and stop mid-quote.
- (new) "chart read October 6" in the sources list is a project note.

**4. Add and cut**

- **Add:** one plain sentence on what a jobs report tells an ordinary person or the Fed (question 2, repeat r0).
- **Cut:** the five comparison sentences ending line 58, from "September's 29,000 is under roughly 50,000" to the last one (new).

