    55	September's 29,000 followed August's revised 133,000. It is below the bureau's average monthly gain of 45,000 over the prior 12 months. The bureau's three-month average is 51,000 a month for the three months through September 2026, against 23,000 for the three months through September 2025. The bureau's payroll series was 158,316,000 in December 2024, 158,432,000 in December 2025, and 159,044,000 in September 2026. That is about 9,700 jobs a month in 2025 and 68,000 a month through September 2026. The Associated Press reported an average 68,000 jobs a month so far this year, against 9,700 average new jobs created every month in 2025.
    56	Break-even employment growth, a Federal Reserve note dated April 2 said, is “the number of jobs that must be added to the economy each month to keep the unemployment rate steady while still absorbing the growth in the potential labor force.” The note says that monthly pace rose to 155,000 in 2023-24 because of a surge in net immigration, then fell to an estimated 85,000 in 2025 as net immigration slowed. It says the 2026 pace could require less than 10,000 new jobs a month, and that the estimates are inherently uncertain. A low break-even means fewer jobs keep the unemployment rate steady. It does not mean hiring is healthy.
    57	The Dallas Fed wrote on March 31 that net unauthorized immigration plummeted after early 2024, with net outflows beginning in February 2025. Christopher J. Waller, a governor of the Federal Reserve Board, said on February 23 that “close to zero net job creation over 2025 indicates a weak, and fragile job market.”
    58	Reuters said economists estimated that the economy needs to create roughly 50,000 jobs per month to keep up with growth in the working-age population. Chris Phelan, chair of the President's Council of Economic Advisers, calculated that the economy needs to add only about 40,000 jobs per month to keep the unemployment rate at its current level. The Associated Press wrote that “the break-even point could now be as low as zero jobs a month, down from 150,000 a year or two ago”. September's 29,000 is under roughly 50,000, the monthly pace Reuters said would keep up with growth in the working-age population. September's 29,000 is under about 40,000, the monthly pace Phelan calculated to keep the unemployment rate at its current level. The bureau's three-month average of 51,000 through September 2026 sits next to roughly 50,000, the monthly pace Reuters said would keep up with the working-age population. September's 29,000 is above zero jobs a month, the break-even the Associated Press said could now be as low as zero. The three-month average of 51,000 is above zero jobs a month, the break-even the Associated Press said could now be as low as zero.
    59	What we do not know yet
    60	The payroll figures for August and September are preliminary: the bureau says they rest on incomplete returns from the employers it surveys. The release states no cause for the 29,000 change. Reuters wrote that economists noted payrolls “have a tendency to underperform when the Labor Day holiday falls relatively late in September, as was the case this year.” September's hiring change is not known yet. The Associated Press wrote that a measure of gross hiring “has been stuck in a rut for more than two years.” The hires rate was 4.2 percent in August 2022, 3.3 percent in August 2024, and 3.3 percent in August 2026. The layoffs and discharges rate was 1.0 percent, 1.1 percent, and 1.0 percent in those months. August's hires were 5.2 million. The September count is scheduled for November 3.
